bash
# Create a test directory with a file
mkdir /tmp/test_clone_demo
cd /tmp/test_clone_demo
echo "Hello World" > original.txt
cat original.txt

# Create a reflink clone (same as pg_upgrade --clone)
cp --reflink=always original.txt clone.txt

# Check inodes (different, just like your PostgreSQL files)
ls -li original.txt clone.txt

# Check physical disk usage before delete
df -h /tmp

# Delete the "original"
rm original.txt

# Check if clone still works!
cat clone.txt  # Still shows "Hello World"!

# Check disk usage - no change because blocks still exist
df -h /tmp

# Clean up
rm clone.txt


The pg14 directory is NOT the "real" data. It's just one of two references to the same physical data.

Think of it like:

A house (physical blocks) with two front doors (pg14 and pg16 directories)

Removing one door doesn't demolish the house

You can still enter through the other door

